**Information Collection and Use**

Most of what the app knows about you stays on your phone. The app does not ask you to create an account, does not ask for an email address or a password, and does not contain advertising.

Information kept on your device only, and never sent to me:

*   Your progress in each region: your best score, your last 10 runs (a score, the time it was played, and for each round the place, how far your pin landed from it and the points it earned), and how many places you have pinned.
*   Your saved places: which places you saved, when you saved them, and when you marked that you had visited them.
*   Your leaderboard player name, if you chose one, so you stay signed in.
*   A cached copy of the game data the app downloaded (the list of regions, the places, their photographs and the stories behind their names), so the game opens quickly and plays with a weak network connection.

This information is removed when you uninstall the app or clear the app's data.

Information sent to the game server:

*   **A player name, only if you choose to sign in to the leaderboard.** Playing does not require it; every mode works without ever entering a name. The name is a name you invent, between 2 and 24 characters. It is not protected by a password and it is shown publicly to other players on the leaderboard, so please do not use your full name, your email address, your phone number, or any other detail that identifies you personally.
*   **Your run scores**, submitted together with that name so the leaderboard can rank them. When you first sign in, the best score you already have on your device for each board is sent as well, so that runs played before you chose a name count.
*   **Requests for game data** (the list of regions, and each region's places, photographs and stories). These carry no information about you beyond what any internet request carries.

Information sent to Google Firebase (usage statistics and crash reports):

The app uses Google Firebase Analytics and Firebase Crashlytics to understand how the game is played and to find and fix crashes. They collect:

*   **Usage statistics**: which screens are opened, and a small set of game events, such as a run starting (with the chosen mode and area), a run ending (with its score and how many rounds scored), the leaderboard or a place's page being opened, the result of a sign-in attempt, and failures to load the game data. The region being played is attached as a property.
*   **Crash reports**: when the app crashes, a report containing the error, the state of the app at the time, and details of the device and operating system version.
*   Together with these, Firebase automatically collects standard information such as a randomly generated app-instance identifier, your device model, operating system version, app version, language, and an approximate country or region derived from your IP address.

What is never sent to Firebase: your player name or anything you type, your location, and your device's advertising identifier. The app switches off Firebase's advertising-ID collection and ad-personalization signals, and does not ask to track you across other apps. This information is not linked to your identity and is not used for advertising. Crash reporting is only active in released versions of the app.

**Log Data**

I want to inform you that whenever your app contacts the game server, the server records standard request information, called Log Data. This Log Data may include your device's Internet Protocol ("IP") address, the request that was made, the time and date of the request, and the software version making it. It is used to keep the service running, to limit abuse, and to diagnose failures, and it is not combined with your player name to build a profile of you.

**Permissions**

The app requests one permission: internet access, used to download game data, show the map, and read and write the leaderboard. It does not ask for and does not access your location, your camera, your microphone, your contacts, or the files on your device. The app never shows or uses where you are; you place your pin on the map yourself.

If you choose to share a place or a finished run, the app opens your phone's system share sheet with a picture and some text. If you pick "save image" in that sheet, your phone may ask for permission to add the picture to your photo library. The app only adds; it never reads your photos.

**Cookies**

Cookies are files with a small amount of data that are commonly used as anonymous unique identifiers. These are sent to your browser from the websites that you visit and are stored on your device's internal memory.

This Service does not use these "cookies" explicitly, and does not contain third-party advertising libraries. Firebase Analytics stores its own app-instance identifier on your device, as described above.

